          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In an elderly patient with multiple chronic conditions, which approach best refl</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In an elderly patient with multiple chronic conditions, which approach best reflects the core principle of geriatric medicine?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Treat the condition with the most abnormal laboratory values first</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Geriatric medicine deliberately moves away from lab-driven prioritisation. The focus is on which condition most threatens the patient's ability to function independently.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Prioritise the condition most threatening the patient's independence and quality of life</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Geriatric medicine reframes the clinical question from 'which lab result is worst' to 'which problem most threatens this person's ability to live independently and with dignity.'</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Refer each condition to the relevant specialist for parallel management</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fragmented specialist-driven care is a common source of polypharmacy and conflicting treatment goals in the elderly. Coordinated, function-centred management is the geriatric approach.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Focus on the most recently diagnosed condition</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Recency of diagnosis does not determine clinical priority in older adults. The guiding criterion is impact on function, independence, and quality of life.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The core principle of geriatric medicine is to prioritise the condition most threatening the patient's independence and quality of life, rather than chasing the worst lab value or following disease-specific guidelines in isolation."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. This reframing — from disease-centred to function-centred care — is the foundation of geriatric medicine and becomes especially important as India's elderly population grows from 140 million to a projected 300 million by 2050."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
