          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which subtype of delirium is most common in hospitalised elderly patients yet mo</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which subtype of delirium is most common in hospitalised elderly patients yet most frequently missed?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hyperactive delirium</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hyperactive delirium, with its visible agitation as seen in Mr Nair pulling out his IV line, is actually the easiest subtype to recognise. The most commonly missed subtype presents very differently.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hypoactive delirium</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Hypoactive delirium presents as quiet withdrawal and reduced alertness, making it easy to mistake for depression or fatigue. Despite being the most common subtype, it is the one most often overlooked.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mixed delirium</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Mixed delirium alternates between agitated and quiet phases. While its quiet phases may be overlooked, pure hypoactive delirium is missed more consistently because it never has an agitated phase to draw attention.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Emergence delirium</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Emergence delirium occurs specifically after general anaesthesia and is a distinct entity. The three geriatric subtypes discussed here are hyperactive, hypoactive, and mixed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Hypoactive delirium is the most common subtype and also the most frequently missed because it presents with quiet withdrawal rather than obvious agitation. Systematic screening with the CAM tool is essential to detect it."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. This is why bedside screening with the Confusion Assessment Method (CAM) is essential — it catches hypoactive delirium that clinical observation alone often misses."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
